{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"prochurch-drowsy-seagull","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# `recheck-review` should accept a `bugfix-size-attestation` block once the ticket carries a current attestation\n\n## The gap, hit for real\n\n`retrigger.py:194` refuses `recheck-review` for ANY current-revision `finding` state, on the reasoning\nthat \"the fix for a finding is amend + re-push, not a retry\" (module docstring, `retrigger.py:15-19`).\nThat is right for a code veto. It is **wrong for `bugfix-size-attestation`**, whose remedy is not a code\nchange at all: the fix is to write the plan into the TICKET and run `rebar review-plan <id>`.\n\nObserved on Gerrit change **1378** (bug `738a-376e-4829-42ca`):\n\n```\n[LLM-Review: BLOCK — finding]\nrebar code review found 1 blocking issue(s):\n- (bugfix-size-attestation) ... 162 non-test line(s) — over the 150-line floor for a bug fix — but\n  bug 738a-376e-4829-42ca has no acceptable plan-review attestation (verdict: unsigned; ...)\n```\n\nThe attestation was then earned (`review-plan 738a` -> PASS, signed; verified `certified` /\n`valid: true`). The finding was therefore **stale**, but:\n\n* `recheck-review` refused it as a \"REAL FINDING\";\n* a no-code-change patchset **copies the -1 forward** (label copy condition\n  `changekind:NO_CODE_CHANGE OR ... TRIVIAL_REBASE`), so re-pushing the same tree does not clear it.\n\nSo the only route was to invent a code change purely to force a re-review. That is exactly the\nchurn-for-the-bot the gate should never require, and it pressures a contributor into cosmetic edits to\nan already-reviewed change.\n\n## Why accepting this case is SAFE\n\nThe module's own security argument already covers it (`retrigger.py:15-19`): a re-review \"can only\nproduce a fresh fail-closed verdict, never mint an unearned PASS\". Refusing `finding` is a\nchurn-control measure, not a safety boundary. If the attestation is genuinely still missing, the fresh\nreview simply re-blocks.\n\n## Design — both inputs stay PRIVILEGED-SIDE\n\nThe module's invariant is that eligibility is decided from durable Gerrit state, \"never from anything\nthe requesting comment says\" (`retrigger.py:13-14`). This condition honours that: it reads the **bot's\nown** vote message plus the **ticket store**. Nothing from the requesting comment is consulted.\n\n1. When the current state is `finding`, parse the bot's own latest vote message on the current revision\n   for its blocking-finding criterion ids (the `- (<criterion-id>) ...` lines).\n2. Proceed only if that set is EXACTLY `{\"bugfix-size-attestation\"}`. Any additional finding still\n   refuses — those do need a code change, and a mixed block must not be unlocked by the attestation.\n3. Extract the ticket id the finding names (`but bug <id> has no acceptable plan-review attestation`;\n   it appears before Gerrit's message truncation).\n4. Re-classify with the gate's OWN privileged-side verifier,\n   `bugfix_size_gate.classify_plan_review_attestation(ticket_id)` — the full chain (op-cert decode ->\n   keyring verify at the storage anchor -> `compute_validity`) — and accept only when\n   `bugfix_size_gate.bucket_for_verdict(verdict) == \"accepted\"`\n   (`ACCEPTED_VERDICTS = {certified, stale-code, stale-head}`).\n\nReusing the gate's own classifier and bucketing is deliberate: a second, independent notion of\n\"acceptable attestation\" in the retrigger path would drift from the gate that produced the finding.\n\n## What must still refuse\n\n* any block whose findings are not exactly `{bugfix-size-attestation}`;\n* an attestation that is absent, `stale-material`, `mismatch`, `invalid`, or otherwise in\n  `FLAG_VERDICTS` — otherwise a contributor could attest, then edit the plan, and unlock a re-review\n  on an attestation that no longer covers the plan;\n* an `infra` verdict (`unavailable` / `error`) must NOT auto-accept via this new arm — it is not\n  evidence the attestation exists. (It remains eligible through the pre-existing coverage-gap arm if\n  the bot itself tagged a coverage gap.)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `recheck-review` is ACCEPTED when the current revision's block is exactly the\n      `bugfix-size-attestation` finding AND the named ticket's plan-review attestation buckets as\n      `accepted` — proven RED-first, since the current code refuses every `finding`\n- [ ] a block containing `bugfix-size-attestation` PLUS any other blocking finding is still REFUSED\n- [ ] a `bugfix-size-attestation` block whose ticket attestation is absent or `stale-material` is\n      still REFUSED, with the observed verdict named in the refusal reply\n- [ ] an `infra` verdict (`unavailable` / `error`) does not accept through this arm\n- [ ] eligibility reads only the bot's own vote message and the ticket store — asserted by a test that\n      puts a forged criterion id and ticket id in the REQUESTING COMMENT and confirms it is ignored\n- [ ] the retrigger path still writes no vote (the existing no-`labels` invariant holds)\n- [ ] `classify_plan_review_attestation` / `bucket_for_verdict` are REUSED, not reimplemented —\n      asserted by a test that monkeypatches the gate's classifier and observes the retrigger honour it\n- [ ] the refusal message for a genuine code finding is unchanged\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit -k \"retrigger or recheck_review or bugfix_size\" -q\n    make lint && make typecheck\n\nRED-first is load-bearing here: the accept path must be shown to fail against today's code (which\nrefuses every `finding`) before the condition is added, or the test proves nothing. The\nforged-comment test is the security assertion — it is the one that would catch a future refactor that\nstarts trusting the requesting comment for the criterion id or the ticket id.","id":"6a81-8ecd-0cd6-41cd","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"recheck-review should accept a bugfix-size-attestation block once the ticket carries a current plan-review attestation"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785873834285996001,"uuid":"f63144ec-264e-490d-874e-7e234fd7f1ff"}